WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.088
- The joint resolution is passed.

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:03.921
(gentle music)

00:00:03.921 --> 00:00:06.030
- [Narrator] Congress approved
legislation that would force

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.460
the adoption of a
tentative labor agreement,

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.950
averting what could have
been a massive rail strike

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.658
involving about 115,000 workers.

00:00:13.658 --> 00:00:16.470
(train horn blaring)

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.140
Unions and railroad carriers
came to an agreement

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.330
in September to avoid a strike.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.480
But four of the 12 rail
unions rejected that contract,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.390
citing specifically a
lack of paid sick days.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.850
But how can Congress
intervene in private contract

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.136
negotiations, forcing
unions to accept a deal?

00:00:33.136 --> 00:00:33.990
(ambient music)

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.270
Thanks to the Railway
Labor Act which was enacted

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.450
in the 1920s, Congress
has the authority to act

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.750
to avoid any interruption
to commerce or operation

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.810
which specifically includes
the settlement of all disputes

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.170
when it comes to things like
pay and working conditions.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.080
Freight Rail moves about
40% of US long distance

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.890
cargo and delivers goods including
coal, grain, and ethanol.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.290
And three-quarters of new cars and trucks

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.020
bought in the US are also moved by rail.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.960
Industry analysis shows that
if a rail strike took place

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.473
the US economy would risk losing
more than $2 billion a day.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.130
President Biden, who has
hailed himself as pro-labor,

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.250
encouraged Congress to
pass the legislation.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.890
- It's not an easy call, but
I think we have to do it.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.630
Economies at risk.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.820
- [Narrator] Members of Congress,
however are divided over

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.380
the decision to intervene.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.050
- It's incumbent upon
Congress to do everything

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.330
that it can to protect these workers.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:31.260
- If you're passing a bill
to force the rail workers

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.430
to work, how strong is your economy?

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:36.840
- [Narrator] This isn't
the first time Congress

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.450
has stepped in, they've done it 18 times.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.700
But not since the early '90s when Congress

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.850
and President George H.W.
Bush passed legislation

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.940
ordering workers back to
their jobs, and then created

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.580
mandatory arbitration
which has long alleviated

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.203
Congress from getting involved.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.850
But this time, because that
arbitration wasn't agreed on

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.380
Congress acted earlier.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.780
Hampering workers' ability to go on strike

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.000
and forcing the acceptance
of the agreement

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.823
which will last until the end of 2024.

